---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/law/foundational/what-makes-a-law">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Law, and it assumes nothing — no cases memorised, no legal
    vocabulary. It walks the whole English legal system (the syllabus's model system) from one
    question: what makes a rule a <em>law</em>? Later lessons revisit each piece of this map in
    depth; here you learn to see the whole machine at once.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>say what distinguishes a law from a command, a custom or a shop rule, and name the stack of sources — constitution, statute, delegated legislation, precedent, custom — that a legal system is built from;</li>
    <li>explain the three ideals around the machine: law and morality, law and justice, and the rule of law;</li>
    <li>trace how Parliament makes an Act, why it delegates law-making, and how delegated law is checked;</li>
    <li>use the four methods judges read statutes with, and explain precedent's grammar — ratio, obiter, distinguishing, overruling;</li>
    <li>describe the two court tracks (civil and criminal), the lay people built into them, the legal personnel who run them, and what access to justice means in practice.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit throughout: whenever you meet a legal rule, ask three questions — who made it,
    under what procedure, and how is it enforced? The answers locate any rule inside the system.
  </p>

  <h2>The shop sign: what makes a rule a law</h2>
  <p>
    A shop window announces "no refunds". A mother tells a child "no biscuits before dinner". A
    fashion spreads until everyone wears it. All three are rules — and none of them is a law.
    What is missing? Not force: the child feels force. Not generality: the fashion applies to
    everyone. What the shop sign lacks is a <em>recognised authority</em> making the rule by
    <em>recognised procedures</em>, and the <em>state's backing</em> behind it. Those are the
    marks of law: a rule made by an authorised institution, applying to everyone in its scope,
    enforceable by the state — and, crucially, made and changed by procedures that are
    themselves law.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="rule or law — file each by what backs it" widget="sort-buckets" config={wmlRuleBuckets} />
  <p>
    That last clause is the deep one. In a legal system, the rule-maker's power has limits set
    by further rules: a parliament that legislates by process, courts that apply rules by
    method, officials whose powers come from enabling acts. "Law" is therefore not one thing but
    a <strong>stack of sources</strong>, each trumping the one below: a
    <strong>constitution</strong> sets the frame; <strong>statutes</strong> are what legislatures
    write; <strong>delegated legislation</strong> is the executive filling in detail;
    <strong>precedent</strong> is what courts said it all means; and custom quietly fills the
    gaps. A lawyer's first skill is knowing which source controls a question — and what to do
    when two seem to conflict.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="stack the sources from top to bottom" widget="order" config={wmlStackOrder} />
  <p>
    Law is also inseparable from <strong>society</strong>: it changes as the society it governs
    changes. Statutes on divorce, privacy and data protection appear nowhere in medieval law
    because the problems are new. And law acts back on society: wearing seatbelts became
    near-universal within a decade of being required, because legal rules both express norms and
    teach them. The relationship runs both directions, and studying it means watching which way
    the causation flows in each case.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="drag the divider — which way does the causation run?" widget="compare" config={wmlSocietyCompare} />

  <h2>Law, morality, justice — and the rule of law</h2>
  <p>
    Three ideals stand next to law — sometimes inside it, sometimes over it. <strong>Law and
    morality</strong>: most law encodes moral judgements (murder is both a crime and a wrong),
    but not all morality is law (lying to a friend), and — the sharpest point — not all law is
    moral, as every unjust statute in history shows. That last case creates law's most famous
    philosophical fight: is an unjust law still law? <strong>Positivists</strong> say yes — law
    is what the recognised process produced, and its validity is one question while its merit is
    another (H. L. A. Hart's position). <strong>Natural-law theorists</strong> say no — a
    genuinely lawless rule fails as law (Lon Fuller, whose "eight ways to fail to make law"
    describe a legal system's internal morality).
  </p>
  <p>
    The related <strong>law and justice</strong> question is different: justice asks whether
    outcomes are <em>fair</em> — and a law can be valid, obeyed and unjust, which is exactly why
    reform machinery exists. The structural answer to both questions is the <strong>rule of
    law</strong>: the principle that government itself operates under law. A. V. Dicey's classic
    three limbs: no one may be punished except for a breach of law established in the ordinary
    courts (<em>no arbitrary power</em>); everyone, however powerful, is subject to the same law
    (<em>equality before the law</em>); and rights are secured by court decisions, not just paper
    declarations. Modern formulations — the World Justice Project's index among them — add
    accessible courts, independent judges and open government. The rule of law is law's own
    conscience, and practically the precondition for the predictability that makes contracts,
    investment and daily life possible.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="true or false — validity, fairness, and the three limbs" widget="judge" config={wmlRuleJudge} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "if it is wrong, it must be illegal — and if it is legal, it
  must be right." Both directions fail. Adultery, lying and ingratitude are legal but widely
  condemned; until reform campaigns succeed, plenty of unjust things are perfectly lawful. The
  overlap between law and morality is large but not total, and the whole subject of legal
  philosophy lives in the gap between the two circles.</p>

  <h2>How law is made: Parliament</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Parliamentary law making</strong> is the system's engine, and the process is
    deliberately slow. A <em>green paper</em> floats policy options for consultation; a
    <em>white paper</em> states the government's chosen policy; then a <em>bill</em> passes
    through stages in both Houses of Parliament — first reading (introduced), second reading
    (debate on principle), committee stage (line-by-line scrutiny and amendments), report and
    third reading — before <em>Royal Assent</em>, the constitutional formality that makes it an
    Act. The friction is the feature: an idea must survive public consultation, two chambers and
    detailed committee scrutiny before it touches anyone's rights.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="line up a bill's stages as it grinds toward an Act" widget="order" config={wmlBillOrder} />
  <p>
    Parliament also delegates. <strong>Delegated legislation</strong> is law made by others
    under powers an Act grants: ministers issue <em>statutory instruments</em> (the technical
    detail of, say, housing benefit rules — thousands per year, far outnumbering Acts), local
    authorities pass <em>by-laws</em>, and the Privy Council issues <em>orders in council</em>.
    Delegation trades democratic scrutiny for speed and expertise — which is why the enabling
    Act is meant to confine it, why a committee (the Joint Committee on Statutory Instruments)
    checks it, and why courts can strike down delegated legislation that goes beyond its parent
    Act (<em>ultra vires</em> — "beyond the powers").
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="find the line that overstates the delegation" widget="spot-error" config={wmlDelegatedSpot} />
  <p>
    When statutes themselves need renewal, the <strong>Law Commission</strong> (a standing body
    since 1965) reviews whole areas of law, consults, and proposes consolidated reform — the
    machinery of planned <strong>law reform</strong> rather than piecemeal patching. And for
    nearly five decades European law sat over all of it: while the UK was an EU member, directly
    applicable EU regulations and directives counted as domestic law, and courts disapplied
    inconsistent statutes. Since Brexit (withdrawal took effect 31 January 2020) that layer
    became <em>retained EU law</em> — kept unless repealed or reformed. The episode's lesson
    outlives it: a legal system's stack can have layers above parliament, and sovereignty
    arguments follow the layering.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> law is rules from a recognised authority by recognised procedure,
    arranged in a stack of sources, judged against morality, justice and the rule of law. The
    next two sections are the lawyer's two working skills on that stack: reading what
    parliament wrote, and following what courts did with it.
  </p>

  <h2>How law is read: statutory interpretation</h2>
  <p>
    Statutes are written in language, and language under-determines cases — so judges need
    methods. The <strong>literal rule</strong> reads the words plainly even if the result is
    absurd: in <em>Whiteley v Chappell</em> (1868) a statute made it an offence to impersonate
    "any person entitled to vote", and a man who impersonated a dead voter walked free — a dead
    person is not entitled to vote. The <strong>golden rule</strong> departs from the plain
    meaning to avoid such absurdity: in <em>Re Sigsworth</em> (1935) a son who had murdered his
    mother was denied the inheritance the statute's plain words seemed to grant. The
    <strong>mischief rule</strong> (from <em>Heydon's Case</em>, 1584) asks what defect the
    statute was passed to cure, and reads it to cure that defect. The modern
    <strong>purposive approach</strong> — strengthened in the UK by the Human Rights Act 1998,
    which requires statutes to be read compatibly with Convention rights where possible — asks
    what purpose the statute serves.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="pair each reading with the case that shows it" widget="match" config={wmlMethodsMatch} />
  <p>
    The methods matter because the same sentence can license any of the four readings, and
    which one wins decides real cases. Alongside the approaches run <em>presumptions</em> and
    internal aids (the long title, schedules) — and a whole craft of reading definitions
    ("includes" versus "means"), exceptions and cross-references. Interpretation is where law's
    claim to be a reasoning discipline is tested daily: not what the judge prefers, but what the
    text, its purpose and the system's methods will bear.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a judge's job is simply to apply the words." If that were
  true, there would be one interpretation rule, not four, and no case would ever be contested
  on meaning. Words carry ambiguity, and legislatures cannot foresee every fact pattern — so
  the system openly equips judges with methods for choosing between readings. The real
  discipline is that the choice must be argued from the text and the system's methods, not from
  the judge's personal preference.</p>

  <h2>How law grows: judicial precedent</h2>
  <p>
    The other great law-making source is the courts themselves, and it works like this:
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="precedent — how one case quietly becomes the law for the next thousand" widget="equationStep" config={precedentCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the power of the sequence is in step 2 — the rule stated in
    one case is <em>compelled</em>, not merely suggested, for later courts. That is
    <em>stare decisis</em>. And the flexibility lives in step 3: because only cases with
    materially similar facts are bound, a later court can show the facts genuinely differ and
    escape the rule. Step 4 is the pressure valve of last resort — overruling — and it is
    reserved to higher courts because predictability is the point of the whole device.
  </p>
  <p>
    Precedent is the common law system's compounding technology: a decision on a new problem (a
    novel technology, a new tort) becomes the rule for the next thousand like cases, and law
    accretes case-by-case over centuries. It has its own grammar: the binding part is the
    <em>ratio decidendi</em> — the rule that justified the outcome — while everything else a
    judge says (<em>obiter dicta</em>, "by the way") only persuades. The hierarchy of courts
    fixes who binds whom: the Supreme Court binds all below it, the Court of Appeal binds itself
    save in narrow exceptions, and every court binds the ones below. <strong>Distinguishing</strong>
    is the safety valve — and it is why the common law can evolve without revolution: the
    doctrine demands consistency, and the craft supplies the exceptions. Precedent's virtues are
    predictability and equality (like cases decided alike); its vice is inertia, which is
    exactly what overruling and the reform machinery exist to check.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="fill the binding part and the by-the-way part" widget="cloze" config={wmlPrecedentCloze} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "if it is not in an Act of Parliament, it is not really law."
  Most of the law you will study began as judges' decisions: murder, contract and negligence
  are largely creatures of precedent, built case by case without any statute. Statute is the
  newest layer of the stack, not the only one — and a well-settled line of cases binds the
  courts exactly as an Act does.</p>

  <h2>The legal system: courts, people, and access</h2>
  <p>
    The machine has two tracks. <strong>Civil courts</strong> settle disputes between parties —
    contract, tort, family money — starting (in England) mostly at the County Court or High
    Court, with appeal upward to the Court of Appeal and Supreme Court. The civil track also
    includes alternatives: tribunals for specialist disputes (employment, immigration), and
    <strong>alternative dispute resolution</strong> — mediation (a neutral helps the parties
    settle), arbitration (a private adjudicator's decision the parties agreed in advance to
    accept) and negotiation — which resolves the large majority of disputes before any judge
    hears them, because trials are slow, public and expensive.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Criminal courts</strong> prosecute offences against the state: summary cases in the
    Magistrates' Court, either-way and indictable cases through the Crown Court, with appeals
    upward. Lay people are built into this track deliberately: <strong>magistrates</strong> —
    unpaid, trained volunteers — decide the vast majority of criminal cases, and the
    <strong>jury</strong> of 12 randomly selected citizens decides guilt in serious trials, the
    system's oldest statement that conviction should require ordinary people's consent.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Legal personnel</strong> run the machinery: solicitors advise and prepare,
    barristers specialise in advocacy and court argument, and the <strong>judiciary</strong> —
    appointed on merit, protected by security of tenure and salary — is the branch whose
    independence the rule of law depends on. A judge who can be dismissed for an unpopular
    ruling is a rule-of-law failure by definition. Finally, <strong>access to justice</strong>:
    rights without the means to enforce them are decoration. The machinery includes legal aid
    (public funding of representation for those who qualify — sharply narrowed by the Legal Aid,
    Sentencing and Punishment of Offenders Act 2012, with consequences still debated), duty
    solicitors, the small-claims track that lets people sue without lawyers, and charities like
    Citizens Advice. Funding is the system's most persistent fault line: a perfect procedure with
    no affordable entry is, for most people, a locked door.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the criminal and civil systems are not two names for one
  thing. They differ in <em>parties</em> (the state prosecutes; private parties sue),
  <em>purpose</em> (punishment versus compensation), <em>outcomes</em> (prison and fines versus
  damages and orders) and <em>standard of proof</em> — beyond reasonable doubt in criminal
  trials, the balance of probabilities in civil cases. O. J. Simpson was acquitted criminally
  but found liable civilly for the same events: not a contradiction, but two different questions
  asked under two different standards.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A minister issues a statutory instrument that goes further than its parent Act allowed. What can a court do, and on what ground?</li>
    <li>A statute's plain words produce an absurd result in a case before a judge. Which two interpretation approaches could the judge consider, and how do they differ?</li>
    <li>A judge writes: "I decide for the claimant because the rule in X applies; I note in passing that a different rule might govern if the contract were written." Which sentence is binding on later courts, and why?</li>
    <li>Why can the same act produce an acquittal in one court and liability in another?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Strike it down as <em>ultra vires</em> — beyond the powers
    the enabling Act conferred. This is the check that keeps delegated legislation subordinate
    to parliament. (2) The golden rule departs from plain meaning to avoid absurdity; the
    purposive approach asks what the statute was <em>for</em> and reads it to serve that purpose
    — the mischief rule is its older cousin, aimed at the specific defect the statute cured.
    (3) Only the first sentence — the <em>ratio decidendi</em>, the rule that justified the
    outcome — binds, because stare decisis binds later courts to the reason for deciding. The
    passing remark is <em>obiter</em>: persuasive, not binding. (4) Because the criminal and
    civil tracks ask different questions under different standards of proof — beyond reasonable
    doubt versus the balance of probabilities — so the same facts can satisfy one and not the
    other.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A law is a rule made by a recognised authority, under recognised procedure, backed by the state — and made by procedures that are themselves law.</li>
    <li>The sources form a stack: constitution, statute, delegated legislation, precedent, custom — knowing which source controls is the lawyer's first skill.</li>
    <li>Law and morality overlap but do not coincide; validity and fairness are separate questions, and the rule of law is the structural answer.</li>
    <li>Parliament's slow multi-stage process is deliberate friction; delegated legislation buys speed and expertise and is checked by ultra vires review.</li>
    <li>Statutes are read by four methods — literal, golden, mischief, purposive — because words under-determine cases.</li>
    <li>Precedent compounds: ratio decidendi binds, obiter persuades; distinguishing is the safety valve, overruling the last resort.</li>
    <li>Civil and criminal tracks differ in parties, purpose, outcomes and standard of proof; access to justice depends on funding, not just rights.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    You now hold the whole map. The next lessons zoom into its regions: how the sources of law
    developed historically, how criminal and civil law differ in substance, and how a case
    actually moves through the courts from claim to appeal. Later, the expert lessons return to
    the questions this lesson could only introduce — what law ultimately <em>is</em>
    (jurisprudence), and how other legal systems arrange the same problems.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three debates run underneath everything above. <strong>Separation of powers</strong>:
    parliament makes law, the executive applies it, courts adjudicate it — and the UK's
    "constitution" enforces the separation not by a single written document but by statutes (the
    Human Rights Act 1998 requiring ministers to declare compatibility with rights; the
    Constitutional Reform Act 2005, which created the Supreme Court and tightened judicial
    appointments) and convention. Courts can declare an Act incompatible with Convention rights —
    but cannot strike it down; parliament remains sovereign, and the pressure that declaration
    generates is the intended remedy.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Hart v Devlin</strong>: when may the criminal law enforce morality? Lord Devlin
    (writing after the 1957 Wolfenden report on homosexuality and prostitution) argued that
    society's shared morality deserves protection like its physical order; H. L. A. Hart argued
    that harm to others, not moral disgust, is the proper trigger — the debate behind every
    subsequent argument about what the criminal law should reach. And <strong>the question the
    whole system exists for</strong>: every rule you will read in this subject's ladder —
    precedent, interpretation rules, appeal routes, juries, legal aid — exists because someone,
    somewhere, was harmed in a way the previous rules could not answer. Law is a moral
    technology under permanent repair, and its mechanics are worth knowing precisely so that the
    repair can be argued for in the system's own language: amendment, appeal, precedent, reform.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Dicey, Introduction to the Study of the Law of the Constitution (1885); Hart, The Concept of Law (1961); Fuller, The Morality of Law (1964); Devlin, The Enforcement of Morals (1965); Glanville Williams, Learning the Law; Law Commission Act 1965; Constitutional Reform Act 2005. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
